{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794913132,"nonce":"/0E4Dcc4Yn/PHJr0ueoRI4JF4HM=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABgAjZaQ","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/200","origin":"github"},"title":"PINN with the documented trial function g + shape.sdf(x, y) * net converges to a wrong field","message":"The geometry guide (`docs/Domain-and-Geometry.md`, \"Signed distance\") recommends\n\n```python\nphi = plate.sdf(x, y)          # 0 on every boundary, hole included\nu   = g + phi * net(x, y)      # u == g on the boundary, for ANY network weights\n```\n\nto make a Dirichlet condition hold exactly, and names a rectangle with a hole as the use case. For a second-order PDE trained on the strong-form residual at collocation points, this converges to a wrong field while the loss falls.\n\nMeasured on release 0.4.2. The guide on `main` still carries the recommendation; not re-measured on `main`.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n`-Laplace(u) = 10` on the rectangle `[0.4, 1] x [0, 1]`, `u = 0` on three sides and a smooth given trace on `x = 0.4`, carried by a C2 lift that vanishes for `x \u003e= 0.6`. MLP with 5 hidden layers of 40 units, 4,000 mesh-free interior points (`shape.domain()` without a size), 1,000 Adam steps (1e-3), then 1,000 self-scaled Broyden steps, double precision. Reference: P1 FEM on a fine mesh. Relative L2 error on 20,000 random points:\n\n| Trial function | After Adam | After quasi-Newton | Loss |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `lift + shape.sdf(x, y) * net` | 0.30 | 0.79 | 177 → 1.8 |\n| `lift + (x - 0.4) * (1 - x) * y * (1 - y) * net` | 0.31 | 2.1e-4 | 126 → 0.024 |\n\nOnly the boundary factor differs between the two runs. A soft boundary term on the same problem (2,000 Adam + 6,000 quasi-Newton steps) reaches 6.6e-5.\n\nWith 4 and 16 circular holes cut from the rectangle, the signed-distance trial function ends at 1.58 and 0.81.\n\n## Likely cause (not confirmed by a run)\n\nThe signed distance of a rectangle, or of a boolean combination, is a min/max of primitive distances. It has kinks where the nearest boundary changes. Across a kink the gradient of `sdf * net` jumps. That jump is a line source that a pointwise residual never samples, so a small residual does not imply a solution.\n\n## Possible fixes\n\n- Say in the guide that the boundary factor must be smooth for strong-form residuals of order two.\n- Offer a smooth boundary factor next to the exact distance (R-function or approximate-distance-function composition).\n- A weak-form residual sees the jump and may not need the restriction; not tested.","files":null}]}